1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,449
Every configuration file on a Linux server is a plain text file.

2
00:00:04,469 --> 00:00:07,565
To change one, you have to create it and edit it,

3
00:00:07,585 --> 00:00:10,108
and the standard editor for that is vim.

4
00:00:10,578 --> 00:00:13,225
In this video: create and edit text files.

5
00:00:17,245 --> 00:00:19,828
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,298 --> 00:00:22,006
Create and edit text files.

7
00:00:22,476 --> 00:00:26,114
We'll create files from the shell, then edit them with vim:

8
00:00:26,134 --> 00:00:29,773
insert text, delete a line, undo, search, and save or quit.

9
00:00:39,293 --> 00:00:43,816
The quickest way to make a file is touch. It creates an empty file.

10
00:00:43,836 --> 00:00:46,121
ls -l shows it is zero bytes long.

11
00:00:52,591 --> 00:00:57,499
To put text in a file without an editor, use echo with the greater-than sign,

12
00:00:57,519 --> 00:01:01,019
as we saw before. It replaces whatever was in the file.

13
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:08,499
Two greater-than signs append instead. They add a line to the end.

14
00:01:08,519 --> 00:01:09,781
cat shows both lines.

15
00:01:17,301 --> 00:01:21,828
vim has two main modes, and this is what confuses everyone at first.

16
00:01:21,848 --> 00:01:26,241
In normal mode, keys are commands. In insert mode, keys type text.

17
00:01:34,711 --> 00:01:38,698
You start in normal mode. Press i or o to enter insert mode.

18
00:01:38,718 --> 00:01:40,434
Press Escape to come back.

19
00:01:40,454 --> 00:01:44,440
If a key does something strange, press Escape and try again.

20
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:59,328
To save and quit, be in normal mode and type a colon.

21
00:01:59,348 --> 00:02:06,818
:w saves. :q quits. :wq does both. :q! quits and throws the changes away.

22
00:02:13,338 --> 00:02:16,716
On Red Hat systems the vi command is always there,

23
00:02:16,736 --> 00:02:21,269
but the full vim command may not be. Here, which vim finds nothing.

24
00:02:21,289 --> 00:02:23,579
Which shows where a command lives.

25
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:35,678
So we install it: sudo dnf install -y vim-enhanced, the same way we installed bzip2.

26
00:02:35,698 --> 00:02:39,952
Most of the output is trimmed. Now which vim finds it.

27
00:02:52,472 --> 00:02:54,902
Now open the file with vim notes.txt.

28
00:02:54,922 --> 00:02:59,802
These are real screens from our lab, recorded from a scripted vim session.

29
00:02:59,822 --> 00:03:01,391
You press the same keys.

30
00:03:06,861 --> 00:03:09,129
Capital G jumps to the last line.

31
00:03:09,149 --> 00:03:13,150
Lowercase o opens a new line below and enters insert mode.

32
00:03:13,170 --> 00:03:15,715
The bottom of the screen says INSERT.

33
00:03:19,685 --> 00:03:20,877
We type line three.

34
00:03:20,897 --> 00:03:25,340
Then Escape returns to normal mode, and the INSERT message disappears.

35
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:34,602
Now :wq saves and quits. And cat confirms the file has three lines.

36
00:03:41,122 --> 00:03:46,459
Open the file again. gg jumps to the first line, and dd deletes the current line.

37
00:03:46,479 --> 00:03:49,038
The first line is gone from the screen.

38
00:03:54,508 --> 00:03:57,389
u undoes the last change, and the line is back.

39
00:04:00,859 --> 00:04:06,210
Now :q! quits without saving, so the file on disk never changed.

40
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:08,391
cat shows all three lines.

41
00:04:14,911 --> 00:04:18,508
A / starts a search. Type /three, and press Enter.

42
00:04:18,528 --> 00:04:21,206
The cursor jumps to the match on line three.

43
00:04:25,676 --> 00:04:32,077
dd deletes that line, and :wq saves. cat shows the file is back to two lines.

44
00:04:38,597 --> 00:04:43,370
Here is the cheat sheet. These keys cover most of what you will do on the exam.

45
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:45,190
The kit has it as a text file.

46
00:05:02,710 --> 00:05:06,146
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

47
00:05:06,616 --> 00:05:11,140
Files on disk survive a reboot. We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

48
00:05:14,610 --> 00:05:17,533
Uptime says zero minutes, so this is a fresh boot.

49
00:05:17,553 --> 00:05:21,182
The file we edited is still here, and the checker confirms it.

50
00:05:26,702 --> 00:05:32,613
Your turn. Use vim to create lab7.txt with three lines: alpha, beta, gamma.

51
00:05:35,583 --> 00:05:40,200
Then delete the beta line, add delta at the end, save, and run the checker.

52
00:05:40,220 --> 00:05:42,240
Try before you open the solution.

53
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:53,751
Next up: Create, delete, copy, and move files and directories.

54
00:05:54,221 --> 00:05:57,123
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
